ie","H2088":"this","H4994":"please","H5493":"remove","H4310":"who?","H4069":"why?","H6213":"do","H3651":"right","H4578":"belly","H1245":"to seek","H5315":"soul","H637":"also","H6258":"now","H1145":"Benjaminite","H3240":"to rest","H194":"perhaps","H7200":"see","H5771":"crime","H2896":"pleasant","H7045":"curse","H3212":"to walk","H1870":"conduct","H1980":"went","H6763":"side","H2022":"mount","H5980":"close","H6080":"to throw","H6083":"dust","H854":"with","H5889":"faint","H5314":"be refreshed","H302":"Ahithophel","H1961":"to be","H2365":"Hushai","H757":"Archite","H7463":"friend","H2421":"to live","H2617":"kindness","H7453":"neighbor","H3808":"not","H977":"to choose","H8145":"second","H589":"I","H5647":"to serve","H6440":"before","H3051":"to give","H6098":"counsel","H6370":"concubine","H8104":"obey","H8085":"hear","H887":"to stink","H2388":"strengthen","H5186":"to stretch","H168":"tent","H1406":"roof","H3289":"to advise","H1992":"they(masc.)","H7592":"to ask","H1697":"word","H430":"God","H1571":"also"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Siba was a mean character, but of sufficient discernment to judge that David would gain the day. He came to calumniate his master; and David paid too much attention to him, though his testimony would not have been received in a court of judicature. (Calmet) — We must reflect that the mind of David was full of trouble, and devoid of suspicion. (Haydock) — But he did wrong (Worthington) in condemning Miphiboseth unheard. — Raisins. See 1 Kings xxv. 18. (Calmet) — Figs; (palatharum) which are often called caricarum. (Menochius) — Hebrew mea kayits, “a hundred of summer” fruits, like fresh grapes, (Numbers xiii. 21.) and other fruits, gathered after harvest time, Micheas vii. 1."],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Loaves. Hebrew, “and to fight.” But the Septuagint and the Masorets reject the letter l, which causes the difference. (Calmet)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Father: a very improbable story, as the son of Jonathan was lame, and all Israel had declared for Absalom. (Menochius)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","All. In the East, crimes are generally punished with the loss of goods. (Calmet) — Kings. He intimates that he had not spoken against his master, with a design to obtain his effects. (Menochius)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Bahurim, a fortress of Benjamin, about an hour’s walk east of Bethania. (Adrichomius xxviii.) — It signifies, “chosen youths;” and it is called Almut, or Almon, “youth,” 1 Paralipomenon vi. 60, &c. Hither Phaltiel conducted Michol, chap. iii. 16. (Calmet)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Belial; contemner of the laws, and murderer. (Menochius)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Dog. David’s nephew was moved with indignation. He could easily have punished the insolence of Semei. (Haydock)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","—11. Hath bid him curse. Not that the Lord was the author of Semei’s sin, which proceeded purely from his own malice, and the abuse of his free-will; but that knowing and suffering his malicious disposition to break out on this occasion, he made use of him as his instrument to punish David for his sins. (Challoner) — He adored the justice of God; who is often said to do what he does not hinder, or what he only permits. (Estius) — David is here a noble figure of Jesus Christ, excusing his executioners, (Haydock) and receiving the insults of the Jews, without complaining. (Calmet) — If Semei had not been guilty of sin, but acted according to God’s will, he could not have been justly punished, 3 Kings ii. (Worthington)"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Affliction, of which he makes a sort of sacrifice, being convinced that God will not reject the contrite and humble heart, Psalm l. 19. (Calmet)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Earth, like a man in fury, Acts xxii. 23."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","There, on the hill side, (Haydock) at Bahurim, ver. 5. (Menochius)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Arachite; perhaps descended from the ancient Arachites, who dwelt near Arad and Tripoli, where the pretended Sabbatic river is said to flow; (Josephus, Jewish Wars xii. 13.) or rather, as the names are written in a different manner, this person might be a native of Arachi, in Benjamin, west of Bethel, Josue xvi. 2. (Calmet) See chap. xv. 32. — Friend. This was his peculiar title of office, 1 Paralipomenon xxvii. 33. (Calmet) — King. (Salve.) Literally, “Hail, O King,” in both places. The salutation is repeated for greater emphasis. (Haydock)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Friend. He rather accuses him of treachery. (Haydock) — But he does not mention the name of king, or of father, lest it should too plainly speak his own condemnation, as an ungrateful rebel. (Salien)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Chosen. (Vox populi, vox Dei) Private people are not commonly able, or allowed, to judge of the right, which the prince has to the throne. But here Absalom was manifestly an usurper; and many still adhered to David. (Calmet) — Chusai assumes the character of a courtier, and flatters the prince; (Salien) who ought to have been on his guard. See chap. xv. 34. (Calmet)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Their hands may be strengthened, &c. The people might apprehend lest Absalom should be reconciled to his father; and therefore they followed him with some fear of being left in the lurch, till they saw such a crime committed, as seemed to make a reconciliation impossible. (Challoner) — This was the most heinous outrage that a son could offer to his father. Jacob resented it to the last, Genesis xlix. 4. Amyntor devoted his son Phœnix to all the furies, for a similar offence. (Homer, Iliad ix.) Armais treated the wives of his brother Sesostris in this manner, when he had resolved to rebel. (Josephus, contra Apion i.)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Israel, who saw him enter the tents, (Calmet) on the flat roof, chap. xi. 2., and xii. 11. (Haydock) — The wives of the conquered king were reserved for the victor. Smerdis married all the wives of his predecessor, Cambyses. (Herodotus iii. 68, 83.) (Calmet)"],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Absalom. It tended to promote the end which was desired, (Haydock) whether good or bad. His prudence is hyperbolically compared with the divine oracles; (Menochius) and his authority must have had great weight, since David began to take precautions, only after he had heard that Achitophel had joined the rebels; and Absalom was persuaded (Calmet) to perpetrate so foul and unnatural a crime publicly, no one daring to make any opposition. Chusai was silent; as he was aware that, if he began to contradict this counsellor at first, he would only incur suspicion. (Haydock) — The unjust commonly endeavour by all means to attach people to themselves: but God, in the end, turns their counsels against themselves. (Worthington)"],["challoner",10,null,"challoner","Hath bid him curse.... Not that the Lord was the author of Semei’s sin, which proceeded purely from his own malice, and the abuse of his free will. But that knowing, and suffering his malicious disposition to break out on this occasion, he made use of him as his instrument to punish David for his sins."],["challoner",21,null,"challoner","Their hands may be strengthened, etc.... The people might apprehend lest Absalom should be reconciled to his father, and therefore they followed him with some fear of being left in the lurch, till they saw such a crime committed as seemed to make a reconciliation impossible."]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"]},"fa":[[9,13,0,"On the Duties of the Clergy","Book 1",0,397,"… He went on as though dumb and humbled; he went on in silence; nor was he disturbed, although called a bloody man, though he was conscious of his own gentleness. He therefore was not disturbed by insults, for he had full knowledge of his own good works.He, then, who is quickly roused by wrong makes himself seem deserving of insult, even while he wishes to be shown not to deserve it. He who despises wrongs is better off than he who grieves over them. For he who despises them looks down on them, as though he doesn't feel them; but he who grieves over them is tormented, just as though he actually felt them."],[9,13,0,"On the Duties of the Clergy","Book 1",0,397,"… Here we have humility; for he thought that those things which are divinely ordered were to be endured with an even mind, as though he were but some servant lad. Then he said, \"Behold, my son, who came forth of my body, seeks my life. Here we have justice. For if we suffer hard things at the hand of our own family, why are we angry at what is done to us by strangers? Lastly he says, \"Let him alone that he may curse, for the Lord has bidden him. It may be that the Lord will look on my humiliation and requite me good for this cursing. …"],[9,12,1,"The Twelve Books on the Institutes of the Coenobia","Book 8",1,435,"… Let him alone that he may curse. For the Lord hath commanded him to curse David. And who is he who shall dare to say, Why hast thou done this? Behold my son, who came forth from my loins, seeks my life, and how much more this son of Benjamin? Let him alone, that he may curse, according to the command of the Lord. It may be the Lord will look upon my affliction, and return to me good for this cursing to-day.\""],[11,12,2,"Homilies on 2 Corinthians","Homily 23",2,407,"… Therefore He saith to Isaiah, \"Comfort ye, comfort ye My people, ye priests: speak unto the heart of Jerusalem, for she hath received of the Lord's hand double for sins. And again; \"Grant us peace, for Thou hast repaid us every thing. And David saith; \"Behold mine enemies, for they are multiplied; and forgive all my sins. And when he bore with Shimei cursing him, he said, \"Let him alone, that the Lord may see my abasement, and requite me good for this day. For when He aideth us not when we suffer wrong, then most of all are we advantaged; for He sets it to the account of our sins, if we bear it thankfully."]],"fau":[["ambrose","Ambrose of Milan",1,"father",null,1,397],["cassian","John Cassian",0,"father",null,0,435],["chrysostom","John Chrysostom",1,"father",null,1,407]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 10 (1896)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf210"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 11 (1894)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf211"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 12 (1889)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf112"]]}